@article{ WOS:001448459200001,
Author = {Campbell, Emma and Donaldson, Ronnie},
Title = {All in the mix: neighbourhood aesthetics, artwashing, and gentrification
   in Salt River, Cape Town},
Journal = {JOURNAL OF HOUSING AND THE BUILT ENVIRONMENT},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {40},
Number = {3},
Pages = {1061-1087},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {This paper offers a look at how neighbourhood aesthetics, public art,
   and housing conditions interact to shape urban transformation. The
   primary aim of the study is to investigate whether artwashing is a
   feature of gentrification in Salt River, a historic neighbourhood near
   Cape Town's central business district. In order to achieve the aim, the
   following objectives were set: to conceptualise artwashing and
   gentrification; to conduct an aesthetic audit of residential homes in
   the study area to document the condition of buildings; to map the
   locations of public artwork in relation to the aesthetic audit; to
   determine residents' opinions on gentrification and urban artwork in
   their neighbourhood; to understand Baz-art's rationale behind the art
   projects in Salt River; and to make policy recommendations to combat
   gentrification in the area. The study employs a mixed-method approach to
   analyse the complex dynamics of residential transformation,
   gentrification, and public art in Salt River. The three main methods
   include a household survey, an aesthetic audit of residential homes, and
   semi-structured interviews with key stakeholders. Salt River, marked by
   its Victorian colonial vernacular architecture, has a unique history,
   escaping the then apartheid government's forced removals in 1950s and
   1960s. Despite historical challenges, recent decades have seen declining
   investment, prompting the City of Cape Town to implement policies like
   the Urban Development Zone and Heritage Protection Overlay Zone to
   uplift and conserve the suburb, respectively. This study utilised a
   mixed-method approach, consisting of three main components First, to
   enhance our understanding of the state of residential transformation in
   Salt River in 2021 the exteriors of residential homes were graded. hos
   aesthetic audit reveals variations in the condition of homes, with upper
   Salt River displaying better ratings than lower Salt River, where most
   mural art is concentrated. Second, the study delved into the motivations
   and consequences of public art projects, particularly those organised by
   Baz-Art. Interviews with Baz-Art's founder, shed light on the permit
   process, community engagement, and the impact on residents' perceptions
   of their environment. Notably, the art distribution correlates with the
   aesthetic ratings, raising questions about its impact on gentrification.
   Third, the study examined residents' opinions through a sampled survey
   and finds a strong connection to the neighbourhood's history and
   resistance to selling properties. However, concerns about environmental
   and social quality, changing neighbourhood character, and outside
   interest are evident. Gentrification is seen as encroaching into
   residential areas, with real estate agents approaching homeowners.
   Community organisations, like the Salt River Heritage Society, play a
   pivotal role in documenting, preserving, and resisting gentrification.
   Despite public art initiatives aiming to bring pride to residents,
   survey results indicate a lack of increased pride among respondents.
   Criticisms include art not reflecting the community's history and themes
   unrelated to Salt River, which resulted in many artworks being defaced,
   and replaced, reflecting local values instead. By linking aesthetic
   audits to gentrification, it opens new avenues for understanding and
   influencing housing policies and urban planning practices that balance
   development with community preservation in Cape Town.},
